  "content": {
    "id": "p40-q3-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "3",
    "prompt": "[Changing direction]\n\nBob claims that to find when a particle changes direction, simply find when it is at rest. In other words, let $v=0$. Mary claims that letting $v=0$ does not guarantee that the particle changes direction. Their teacher gives them both the function $x=t^3-3t^2+3t$ to investigate.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p40-q3-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find the velocity-time function.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$v=3t^2-6t+3$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}v&=\\frac{dx}{dt}\\\\v&=3t^2-6t+3\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 12
      },
      {
        "id": "p40-q3-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find when the particle is at rest by letting $v=0$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$t=1$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}3t^2-6t+3&=0\\\\3(t-1)^2&=0\\\\t&=1\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 16
      },
